.\" Man page for ax25tcpd
.\"
.TH AX25TCPD 8 "11 Aug 2026" "ax25-apps" "Linux System Administration"
.SH NAME
ax25tcpd \- interactive AX.25 frontend to an AGWPE multiplexer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ax25tcpd
.RB [ \-c
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-C
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ax25tcpd
is an interactive frontend to an
.BR ax25netd (8)
loop port.  Each client connection is a session: the first line is a
command, the bytes after it are packet data.  It is useful for scripts
and for talking to AX.25 services by hand.
.SH PORTS
Every
.B listen tcp
line creates two TCP listeners.  The binary port
.I X
passes the raw stream: everything sent after the command line is
accumulated and delivered as one packet when the connection closes,
split into
.B mtu
sized chunks.  The text port
.I X+1
sends each CR, LF or CRLF terminated line as one packet.  A
.B listen unix
socket behaves like a text port.
.SH COMMANDS
Unambiguous prefixes of the commands are accepted.  The end of a
session is signalled by closing the connection, or, on the text ports,
by the
.B quit
or
.B bye
commands.
.TP
.BI "connect [" options "] " port [ : chan ] " " dest [, digi , ...] " [< SRC]"
Start an AX.25 connection.  The port is a name from the netd port list,
optionally with a channel number, or a flat port number;
.B loop
names the virtual loopback port.  While connecting, input is held and
delivered once the connection is up.  On the binary port the connection
ends when the client closes the connection; on the text ports it ends
when the remote disconnects, or after
.B quit
.
.TP
.BI "datagram [" options "] " port [ : chan ] " [" dest [, digi , ...] "] " [< SRC]"
Send unconnected (UI) frames.  On the binary port the whole remaining
stream is one datagram, split into mtu sized chunks when the connection
closes; on the text ports every line is one frame.
With a
.IR dest
the header is fixed for the whole session and every line is payload.
Without one, every line is a whole TNC2 frame
.RB ( "SRC>DEST,DIGI,...:payload" )
whose source and path come out of the line itself.
.PP
Commas and spaces both separate a path, and mix freely:
.I "DB0AAA-8 DB0BBB"
means the same as
.IR "DB0AAA-8,DB0BBB" .
.PP
Replies are written in the client's own line convention: a client whose
lines end with a carriage return (CR or CRLF) gets CRLF replies, any
other gets bare LF.  Data is never rewritten.
.TP
.B quit
.BR bye
Close the session.
.TP
.B help
List the commands.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-silent
Suppress the "*** CONNECTED" and "*** DISCONNECTED" status lines.
.TP
.B \-\-keep
Do not end the session when a connection closes; the command prompt
returns and a new connect or datagram may be issued.
.TP
.BI \-\-pid= XX
Set the protocol identifier.  Accepts
.BR text ,
.BR netrom ,
.BR rose ,
.BR flexnet ,
.BR l3
or a number.  The default is text (0xF0).
.TP
.BI \-\-port " port"
Give the port separately, allowing the destination as the first
argument.
.TP
.BI \-\-mycall " call"
Give the source call, exactly like the
.B < SRC
argument.
.SH SOURCE CALL
The source call comes from the optional
.B < SRC
argument, from
.B \-\-mycall
or from the
.B default-call
directive in
.BR ax25tcpd.conf (5).
Without either, the command is refused.
.SH GENERAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " file"
Use
.I file
instead of the default configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-C " file"
Shared loop port configuration, default
\fI@@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25common.conf\fR; the back side is taken
from it (see
.BR ax25common.conf (5))
unless a
.B target
line overrides it.
.TP
.B \-f
Stay in the foreground.
.TP
.B \-d
Log to standard error.
.SH FILES
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25tcpd.conf
.br
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25common.conf
(back side unless overridden)
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ax25tcpd.conf (5),
.BR ax25netd (8),
.BR ax25common.conf (5),
.BR agwpe.conf (5)
